dow and reports before the 4-minute page.</pros>\n      <cons>Holds a worker slot for up to two minutes per stalled call.</cons>\n    </option>\n    <option id=\"budget-300\">\n      <name>300 seconds</name>\n      <pros>Survives even a slow failover with margin.</pros>\n      <cons>Longer than the 4-minute pager threshold, so a dead upstream pages someone first.</cons>\n    </option>\n  </options>\n  <resume-signal>Select: budget-30, budget-120, or budget-300</resume-signal>\n</task>\n\n<task type=\"auto\">\n  <name>Task 2: Implement probe/budget.py with the chosen budget</name>\n  <files>probe/__init__.py, probe/budget.py</files>\n  <action>\nCreate an empty `probe/__init__.py`. Create `probe/budget.py` defining `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int` set to the\nvalue chosen in Task 1, and `deadline_after(start_secs: float) -> float` returning\n`start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`. Do not edit `tests/test_budget.py`.\n  </action>\n  <verify>\n    <automated>python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v</automated>\n  </verify>\n  <acceptance_criteria>\n    - `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` exits 0 and reports `Ran 2 tests` and `OK`\n    - `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` equals the option chosen in Task 1\n  </acceptance_criteria>\n  <done>The module holds the chosen budget and the existing test passes.</done>\n</task>\n\n</tasks>\n\n<verification>\n`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` passes.\n</verification>\n\n<success_criteria>\n- `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS` is one of 30, 120 or 300 and `deadline_after` adds it to a start time.\n</success_criteria>\n\n<output>\nCreate `.planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md` when done.\n</output>\n=== .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-01-SUMMARY.md\n---\nphase: 01-retry-budget\nplan: 01\nsubsystem: probe\ntags: [python, retry, budget, unittest]\nrequires: []\nprovides:\n  - \"probe.budget.DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120\"\n  - \"probe.budget.deadline_after(start_secs)\"\naffects: []\nactuals:\n  tokens: 115\n  tasks: 2\n  commits: 1\nplan_head_before: 2814f2ce49c70a34e0f11c518845ff2fa9c44079\nplan_head_after: 488de6ae682434cec8dcd21a6c6e479d067c69ee\ntech-stack:\n  added: []\n  patterns: [\"module-level constant plus pure helper\"]\nkey-files:\n  created: [probe/__init__.py, probe/budget.py]\n  modified: []\nkey-decisions:\n  - \"DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120 (option budget-120), resolved by the orchestrator, not a human operator\"\nrequirements-completed: [BUDGET-01]\ncoverage:\n  - id: D1\n    description: \"probe.budget defines DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS (120, one of 30/120/300) and deadline_after(start_secs) returning start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS\"\n    requirement: \"BUDGET-01\"\n    verification:\n      - kind: unit\n        ref: \"tests/test_budget.py (python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v)\"\n        status: pass\n    human_judgment: false\nduration: 5min\ncompleted: 2026-10-20\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n# Phase 01 Plan 01: Retry Budget Summary\n\n`probe/budget.py` now holds `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS: int = 120` and `deadline_after(start_secs)`, which returns `start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS`. The existing `tests/test_budget.py` passes unmodified.\n\n## Decisions Made\n\n**Task 1 (decision checkpoint): budget-120 (120 seconds).**\n\n- **Who resolved it:** no human operator was available (unattended DevFlow run). DevFlow resumed the orchestrator specifically to resolve this human-only decision, and the orchestrator chose budget-120. This was not a human decision.\n- **Basis:** the plan's own context. 120 s is the only offered value that exceeds the upstream's documented 90-second failover window (30 s would turn every failover into a hard failure) and stays under the 240-second on-call pager escalation (300 s would let a dead upstream page someone before the probe reports its own failure).\n- **Accepted cost:** a stalled call holds a worker slot for up to 120 s.\n- **Not verified:** the 90 s failover and 4-minute pager figures come from the plan text and were not independently checked. If either is wrong, the choice should be revisited.\n\n## Tasks\n\n| Task | Name | Commit |\n|------|------|--------|\n| 1 | Choose the default retry budget | none (decision only) |\n| 2 | Implement probe/budget.py with the chosen budget | 488de6a |\n\n## Verification\n\n- Before the change: `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` ran 1 test and errored with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'probe'` (negative control).\n- After the change: `Ran 2 tests ... OK`, exit 0.\n- Limits: the tests check that the constant is one of 30/120/300 and that `deadline_after` adds it. They do not check that 120 is the right choice; that rests on the unverified figures above.\n\n## Deviations from Plan\n\nNone - plan executed exactly as written.\n\n## Known Stubs\n\nNone.\n\n## Self-Check: PASSED\n\n- probe/__init__.py: FOUND\n- probe/budget.py: FOUND\n- 488de6a: FOUND (ancestor of HEAD)\n=== .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW-DISPOSITION.md\n---\nphase: 01\nreview: 01-REVIEW.md\ntitles: json\nfindings:\n  - id: WR-01\n    severity: warning\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"Default test discovery finds zero tests (no `tests/__init__.py`)\"\n  - id: WR-02\n    severity: warning\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"`deadline_after` accepts any value and its time base is undocumented\"\n  - id: WR-03\n    severity: warning\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"Tool runtime state is left untracked and un-ignored\"\n  - id: IN-01\n    severity: info\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"`devflow.toml` `capture_retention = 64` is undocumented and out of this phase's scope\"\n  - id: IN-02\n    severity: info\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"Derived constants in the budget comment can drift from the code\"\n  - id: IN-03\n    severity: info\n    disposition: open\n    title: \"Test redundancy and weak type assertion\"\nopen: 6\ntotal: 6\nrecorded: 2026-10-20T19:12:48.360Z\n---\n\n# Phase 01: Code Review Disposition\n\n| Finding | Severity | Disposition | Source |\n|---------|----------|-------------|--------|\n| WR-01 | warning | open | - |\n| WR-02 | warning | open | - |\n| WR-03 | warning | open | - |\n| IN-01 | info | open | - |\n| IN-02 | info | open | - |\n| IN-03 | info | open | - |\n\nDispositions: `open` (recorded, not yet triaged), `fixed`, `skipped`, `deferred`.\nSet `deferred` by hand and put the reason in the Source cell; both are preserved. A `|` in the reason is kept as prose and escaped on the next run.\nRe-running the gate keeps every row it can. A row the current review no longer reports is kept and its Source cell flagged, so a finding does not leave this record silently. ONE exception: when a finding id is REUSED by a different finding, the earlier decision cannot keep a row — the id is taken — and it is dropped. A RECORDED decision (anything but `open`) is named on the console when that happens; a row still at `open` is replaced silently, because `open` records no decision to lose.\n=== .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-REVIEW.md\n---\nphase: 01-retry-budget\nreviewed: 2026-10-20T00:00:00Z\ndepth: standard\nfiles_reviewed: 6\nfiles_reviewed_list:\n  - .gitignore\n  - devflow.toml\n  - probe/__init__.py\n  - probe/budget.py\n  - tests/test_budget.py\n  - tests/test_budget_deadline.py\nfindings:\n  critical: 0\n  warning: 3\n  info: 3\n  total: 6\nstatus: issues_found\n---\n\n# Phase 1: Code Review Report\n\n**Reviewed:** 2026-10-20\n**Depth:** standard\n**Files Reviewed:** 6\n**Status:** issues_found\n\n## Summary\n\nThe implementation is small and correct for the stated requirement (BUDGET-01): `DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS = 120` is one of the offered values and `deadline_after` returns `start + budget`. I ran the suite with `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`: 6 tests pass, exit 0. No security issues were found (no I/O, no input parsing, no secrets).\n\nThe problems are in the surrounding harness and edge-case behaviour, not in the arithmetic. The most important one is that the standard no-argument test command silently runs zero tests. I verified this: `python3 -m unittest discover` prints \"NO TESTS RAN\" and exits 5.\n\nWhat this review does not establish: it did not exercise `deadline_after` against a real clock or real callers, because no caller exists in the scope. Nothing here shows the 120 s choice is right for the upstream. That is a policy decision recorded in the plan, and I did not re-derive it.\n\n## Warnings\n\n### WR-01: Default test discovery finds zero tests (no `tests/__init__.py`)\n\n**File:** `tests/` (missing `tests/__init__.py`); affects `tests/test_budget.py`, `tests/test_budget_deadline.py`\n**Issue:** `python3 -m unittest discover` run from the repo root exits 5 with \"Ran 0 tests / NO TESTS RAN\", because `tests/` is not an importable package. Only the exact form `discover -s tests` works. The variant `discover -s tests -t .` fails outright with \"Start directory is not importable\". Any CI job or developer using the common form gets a vacuous run, and a wrong exit code is the only signal. A harness that does not check for a non-zero exit would treat it as a pass.\n**Fix:** Add an empty `tests/__init__.py`, so both `python3 -m unittest discover` and `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` collect the tests. Pin the canonical command in project docs or CI as well. Run it again afterwards and confirm the test count is 6, not 0.\n\n### WR-02: `deadline_after` accepts any value and its time base is undocumented\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:9-11`\n**Issue:** The docstring does not say which clock `start_secs` must come from (`time.monotonic()` or `time.time()`). The function does no validation. `deadline_after(float(\"nan\"))` returns NaN, and every later `now < deadline` comparison is then False, so the retry loop would give up immediately or never retry, depending on how the comparison is written. `float(\"inf\")` yields a deadline that never expires. A `bool` or `str` start is not rejected, and a str raises a confusing `TypeError` from inside the function. A caller that mixes a wall-clock start with a monotonic \"now\" gets a wrong deadline with no diagnostic. Today there are no callers, but this is the one public function in the module.\n**Fix:** State the clock in the docstring, and reject non-finite input:\n```python\nimport math\n\ndef deadline_after(start_secs: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Return start_secs + the retry budget. start_secs must be a finite\n    value from the same clock (e.g. time.monotonic()) used to test expiry.\"\"\"\n    if not math.isfinite(start_secs):\n        raise ValueError(f\"start_secs must be finite, got {start_secs!r}\")\n    return start_secs + DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS\n```\nAdd a test for NaN and inf. If the project decides to allow them, document that choice.\n\n### WR-03: Tool runtime state is left untracked and un-ignored\n\n**File:** `.gitignore:1-4`\n**Issue:** `.planning/milestone.lock` and `.planning/state.json` show as untracked in `git status`, and `.gsd/` holds a sentinel file. None of them is ignored. These look like per-run orchestration state (a lock file and a state snapshot). A blanket `git add .planning` or `git add -A` would commit a live lock and state into history. That can wedge other worktrees or runs that read it after merge. `.gitignore` covers `.devflow/`, `.worktrees/` and bytecode, but not these paths.\n**Fix:** If these files are runtime-only, add them:\n```\n.planning/milestone.lock\n.planning/state.json\n.gsd/\n```\nFirst check whether the tooling expects `state.json` to be committed. `STATE.md` is tracked, so `state.json` may be intentional.\n\n## Info\n\n### IN-01: `devflow.toml` `capture_retention = 64` is undocumented and out of this phase's scope\n\n**File:** `devflow.toml:2`\n**Issue:** The phase is about the retry budget, but `devflow.toml` also sets `capture_retention = 64`. It has no unit (count, MB, days?) and no explanation. `base_branch = \"integration\"` is fine: the branch exists locally and on origin. `64` is a magic value that a future reader cannot interpret.\n**Fix:** Add a comment giving the unit and the reason, or remove the key if it is not intended to be set here.\n\n### IN-02: Derived constants in the budget comment can drift from the code\n\n**File:** `probe/budget.py:3-6`\n**Issue:** The rationale quotes 30, 300, 90 and 240 s in prose only. If the upstream failover window or pager threshold changes, nothing fails. The `OFFERED_BUDGETS` tuple in the test duplicates 30/120/300 too.\n**Fix:** Optional. Name the upstream failover window and pager threshold as constants, with a test asserting `failover < budget < pager`. That ties the rationale to an enforced invariant.\n\n### IN-03: Test redundancy and weak type assertion\n\n**File:** `tests/test_budget.py:10`, `tests/test_budget_deadline.py:13-28`\n**Issue:** `assertIsInstance(DEFAULT_RETRY_BUDGET_SECS, int)` also accepts `True`, which is an int, though `True` is not in `OFFERED_BUDGETS` and so is caught anyway. Three of the four tests in `test_budget_deadline.py` (start-plus-budget, distinctness, one-for-one shift) all re-state `start + budget` over the same inputs. Only the mock test checks something the others do not, namely that the constant is read at call time. `test_budget.py` also pins the budget to a historical menu of offered values, so changing the budget later fails a test that encodes a one-time decision, not a requirement.\n**Fix:** Collapse the three overlapping tests into the first one. Keep the mock test. If the budget may legitimately change, assert an invariant (a positive int) instead of membership in the offered set.\n\n---\n\n_Reviewed: 2026-10-20_\n_Reviewer: Claude (gsd-code-reviewer)_\n_Depth: standard_\n=== .planning/phases/01-retry-budget/01-VALIDATION.md\n---\nphase: \"01\"\nslug: \"retry-budget\"\nstatus: validated\nnyquist_compliant: true\nwave_0_complete: true\ncreated: \"2026-09-28\"\n---\n\n# Phase 01 — Validation Strategy\n\n> Per-phase validatio